course: dictionary
lesson: 5632
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Limpet is one of them. In a science text, a limpet is a small sea animal with a cone-shaped shell that clings very tightly to rocks. «Limpets graze algae under water and clamp tight when the tide goes out» tells you this is about the seashore.
explain: Context shows you which meaning the writer wants. Look at the words around the word. Rock pools, tides and algae mean the science meaning. Words about a person sticking close to you mean the everyday meaning.
explain: In everyday talk, «cling like a limpet» means to hold on very tightly. Someone who sticks to you like a limpet will not leave you alone. Always check: put your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: A limpet is a small sea animal with a cone-shaped shell.
fact: Limpets cling very tightly to rocks, and they clamp tight when the tide goes out.
fact: «Limpets» live on rocks by the sea, in the same rock pools as crabs and anemones.
fact: In informal talk, a limpet is someone who sticks to you and will not leave you alone.
exercise: «Limpets clamp down on the rocks when the tide goes out.» What is a limpet? | *A small sea animal with a cone-shaped shell | A kind of seaweed | A bird that nests on cliffs | A fish that swims very fast
exercise: Dad says, «My little sister sticks to me like a limpet.» What does he mean? | She has a hard shell on her back | She has swum away from the beach | *She holds on to me and won't leave me alone | She lives under the sea
exercise: «The seashore is a harder home: limpets clamp down, anemones fold into blobs of jelly and crabs hide under weed.» Which animal clamps down? | Anemones | *Limpets | Weed | Crabs
test: What does a limpet's cone-shaped shell help it do? | Dig holes in the sand | Fly over the sea | *Cling tightly to rocks | Float on the waves
test: Which sentence uses limpet in the everyday, informal meaning? | A limpet has a cone-shaped shell | Limpets live on rocks by the sea | *Tom followed me everywhere all day, like a limpet | The limpets clamped tight when the tide went out
exam: Jayden says limpet only ever means a sea animal, so he can skip the other meaning. Is he right? | Yes, it only means a sea animal | Yes, it only means a type of rock | *No, in everyday talk it can mean someone who sticks to you | No, it means the tide going out
